      "writtenOn": "2026-11-17T01:54:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To clarify, this change doesn’t add any UI fields. Users can paste an `authorized_keys` entry containing the `expiry-time` option into the existing SSH key field, and Gerrit will honor it:\n\n```text\nexpiry-time\u003d\"20300102Z\" ssh-rsa AAAA... comment\n```\n\nThis series also implements a maximum key lifetime and rejects an expiry that exceeds it when the key is added. Would you prefer dropping this change from the series \n\nHowever, your comment also raises a case I hadn’t considered: the server policy could change afterward. Should we instead record an `add-time` or `create-time` option in Gerrit’s stored `authorized_keys` entries rather than `expiry-time` which is a standardized option ?",
      "parentUuid": "254c2166_a2b68da5",
      "revId": "ac065ee8f54ac2547bd318034e380f3385e1b19c",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
